\vspace{-4mm}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-3mm}

\looseness=-2 Recursive generation produces a decomposable depth response---location features drift while shape is approximately preserved---enabling cross-generator depth classification (F1\,=\,0.694--0.762) within the base-model scope.
Cross-model chains confirm generalization across architectures (F1\,=\,0.830; ANOVA: depth explains ${>}48{\times}$ more variance than generator identity).
QLoRA recovery (F1\,=\,0.89--0.91) suggests tractable extension to instruct models; DDR connects depth profiling to model collapse analysis and corpus auditing (batch estimation: ${\pm}3$--$4$\% at $n{=}1{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis}).

\vspace{-1mm}
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
\vspace{-1mm}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_transfer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-generator depth classification: $8{\times}8$ transfer matrix (binary F1, gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+). Each cell shows F1 when training on one generator (row) and testing on another (column). Mean cross-generator F1\,=\,0.810 [95\% CI: 0.796, 0.822]; uniformly high off-diagonal values demonstrate that depth signal transfers robustly across five architectural families without per-generator calibration.}
\label{fig:transfer-heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Depth Classification Performance}
\label{sec:results-classification}

The 7-feature ensemble achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.762 (95\% CI [0.758, 0.766]; $N$\,=\,37{,}058) across eight generators from five families (Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}), with cross-generator F1 spread of 9.1\,pp capturing generator selection uncertainty.
This 3-class metric is inflated by shared gen-0 seeds across generators (per-class gen-0 F1\,$\approx$\,0.94; \S\ref{sec:generation}); we therefore report \textbf{leak-free binary F1} (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+, our primary conservative metric)\,=\,0.694 (CI [0.688, 0.700]; generator-level mean${\pm}$SE: $0.694{\pm}0.010$) throughout, excluding gen-0 entirely.
Per-generator binary F1 ranges from 0.633 (Mistral-7B) to 0.724 (Pythia-2.8B; SD\,=\,0.028), with the primary difficulty at the gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ boundary (Appendix~\ref{app:confusion}).

\paragraph{Cross-model generalization.}
Cross-model chains (randomly selected generator per step) yield binary F1\,=\,0.830 (95\% CI [.818, .841]; Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-detail}) under 5-generator LOGO.\footnote{Pythia-2.8B/1B, OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B/1.7B; generator-level LOGO with bootstrap CIs ($n$\,=\,1{,}000). Not directly comparable to 8-generator LOGO due to differing subsets.}
Single-generator transfer (Appendix~\ref{app:transfer}) confirms the signal is not a mixed-chain artifact (mean transfer F1\,=\,0.810, Figure~\ref{fig:transfer-heatmap}; LOFO\,=\,0.686); the cross-model advantage is only 2\,pp; ANOVA variance decomposition confirms generator identity contributes ${<}1$\% of feature variance ($\eta^2_{\text{gen}} \leq 0.009$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-detail}).
A scale effect emerges: smaller models achieve higher F1 (Pythia-2.8B: 0.724 vs.\ Mistral-7B: 0.633), partly reflecting same-family affinity with the Pythia-410M reference (spread $\leq$0.013; \S\ref{sec:results-differential}).
An end-to-end QLoRA classifier reaches 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 ($+$11.6\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:qlora}), confirming additional depth signal in token-level representations; on instruct data, QLoRA recovers to 0.89--0.91 (Appendix~\ref{app:instruct}), establishing the decomposition as DDR's primary contribution.

\paragraph{Confound controls.}
Length-matched subsampling yields binary F1\,=\,0.724 (vs.\ 0.770; Appendix~\ref{app:length}); independent-seed evaluation with disjoint pools (F1\,=\,0.680; Appendix~\ref{app:seed-control}) confirms the classifier captures generative degradation, not seed-specific content.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{8-generator LOGO macro-F1 for the 7-feature ensemble (bootstrap 95\% CIs, $n$\,=\,1000). Binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) is the primary metric; it excludes gen-0 entirely and is unaffected by seed sharing. Cross-generator binary F1: SD\,=\,0.028, IQR\,=\,[0.692, 0.714]. $^{\dagger}$3-class F1 carries upward bias because gen-0 seeds are shared across generators (\S\ref{sec:generation}); independent-seed controls confirm the depth signal (F1\,=\,0.680; Appendix~\ref{app:seed-control}). CIs: text-level bootstrap ($n$\,=\,1{,}000) within each fold.}
\label{tab:logo-results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{3-class F1}$^{\dagger}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-2.8B     & Pythia  & 2.8B & \textbf{0.724} & [.707, .742] & 0.798 \\
Pythia-1B       & Pythia  & 1.0B & 0.718 & [.700, .735] & \textbf{0.806} \\
Qwen3-1.7B     & Qwen    & 1.7B & 0.709 & [.693, .726] & 0.772 \\
Llama-3.2-3B   & Llama   & 3.2B & 0.697 & [.680, .713] & 0.775 \\
OLMo-2-1B      & OLMo    & 1.5B & 0.695 & [.679, .711] & 0.781 \\
Qwen3-8B       & Qwen    & 8.2B & 0.692 & [.674, .708] & 0.714 \\
Qwen3-0.6B     & Qwen    & 0.6B & 0.691 & [.673, .709] & 0.780 \\
Mistral-7B     & Mistral & 7.0B & 0.633 & [.617, .648] & 0.682 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{Average (8-gen LOGO)}} & \textbf{0.694} & [.688, .700] & \textbf{0.762} \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{LOFO (family-level)}} & 0.686 & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.pdf}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 vs.\ inference cost in ms/text (8-gen pooled). The 7-feature ensemble (one 410M reference model) matches multi-detector ensembles (two models, 1.4B total) at ${\sim}2.4\times$ lower cost; individual detectors vary widely when repurposed for depth. Inference costs measured on a single NVIDIA RTX 5090.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Formal definition.}
For feature $f$ measured across depths $d \in \{0,1,2{+}\}$, we define its \emph{depth sensitivity} as $\text{DDR}(f) = |\rho(\text{depth}, f)|$, where $\rho$ is Spearman correlation over all per-text measurements.
A feature is classified as \emph{drifting} if Cohen's $d > 0.50$ between adjacent depths, or \emph{stationary} if $d < 0.15$; these thresholds are reference anchors, not definitive boundaries---the two-cluster structure is robust across $\varepsilon \in [0.10, 0.75]$, supported by unsupervised $k$-means (silhouette\,=\,0.660, $p{=}0.030$), exhaustive enumeration of all 35 splits (this partition ranks first), and independent replication in all eight generators (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}); crucially, each generator's data is fully independent, making per-generator split analysis an 8-fold independent validation of the partition rather than same-data circularity.
Recursive inputs grow increasingly self-typical~\citep{markovian_chains}, predicting location drift with shape invariance (confirmed across nucleus, greedy, and top-$k$ strategies; Appendix~\ref{app:decoding-strategy}).

Figure~\ref{fig:ddr-trajectory} reveals the dichotomy: central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) decline monotonically with depth, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay, entropy min) remain flat.
The partition is binary ($k{=}2$; silhouette\,=\,0.660), with entropy std as a sole \emph{intermediate} member ($d{=}0.46$, $|\rho|{=}0.16$), distinct from three near-stationary features ($d < 0.10$; Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response (DDR). Drifting features (blue; $|\rho| > 0.60$, effect size $d{=}0.74$--$1.05$) shift monotonically with generation depth; near-stationary features (amber; $d \leq 0.10$) are approximately preserved; entropy std (wine; $d{=}0.46$) is intermediate. Diamonds: cross-generator means; dots: per-generator values.}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled Spearman correlations corroborate the split: drifting features show $|\rho| > 0.60$ (hapax ratio $-0.64$, TTR $-0.62$, entropy mean $-0.60$) vs.\ $|\rho| \leq 0.16$ for entropy max and tail decay; entropy min reaches $|\rho|{=}0.40$ due to its gen-0/gen-1 boundary sensitivity (discussed below)---a persistent magnitude gap defining the DDR partition (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}).

\paragraph{Quantification.}
Cohen's $d$ \cite{cohen1988statistical} shows a ${\geq}8{\times}$ gap between drifting ($d{=}0.74$--$1.05$) and near-stationary ($d < 0.10$); KS, Glass's $\Delta$, Cliff's $\delta$ all corroborate (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).
Entropy min and tail decay are \emph{boundary features}: entropy min is near-stationary for gen-1/gen-2+ ($d{=}0.016$) but sensitive at gen-0/gen-1; tail decay passes TOST for only 4/8 generators ($|d|{=}0.10$--$0.22$).
\paragraph{Validation.}
Each hold-out generator independently recovers the identical partition (silhouette 0.505--0.760; Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}); TOST confirms near-stationarity for entropy min (8/8) and entropy max (6/8); permutation testing confirms the split as non-random ($p{=}0.030$); $\Delta|\rho| \leq 0.05$ across splits; disjoint generator-pool splits, heteroscedasticity robustness, per-feature convergence dynamics (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}), and per-family DDR consistency (Appendix~\ref{app:family-ddr}) appear in Appendices~\ref{app:holdout-split}--\ref{app:convergence}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_convergence_dynamics.pdf}
\caption{Feature convergence dynamics across generation depth. Drifting features (blue) show monotonically increasing effect size ($|d| > 1$ by gen-2+); near-stationary features (amber) remain within $|d| < 0.25$ at all depths, confirming shape stability is not an artifact of insufficient chain length.}
\label{fig:convergence}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773, 10-fold stratified CV, complementing the LOGO protocol in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}) outperforms surface-only (0.688) and reference-only (0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablation-table}).
The collinear drifting triplet captures ${\sim}$1 effective dimension (PCA PC1\,=\,92.3\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-feature-correlation}); group ablation confirms complementarity for depth profiling: 3-class F1 gains $+$5.3\,pp when non-drifting features are added (drifting-only 0.716, all-7 0.769), while binary detection is already saturated by location drift alone (drifting-only binary F1\,=\,0.697 vs.\ all-7\,=\,0.700; Appendix~\ref{app:ablation-table})---shape stability contributes to depth \emph{grading}, not detection.
TTR and hapax ratio ($r = 0.97$) are retained for marginal LOFO gains \cite{strobl_conditional_2008} (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) yield F1 spread of only 0.013 (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-ref}); same-family proximity may inflate Pythia F1 by up to 1.3\,pp, but the DDR partition is preserved.

\paragraph{Temperature and prompt-type sensitivity.}
All four tested generators degrade at $t{=}0.9$ ($-$9.3 to $-$26.0\,pp binary F1; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}); continuation chains show the strongest drift (F1\,=\,0.781; Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}).
The DDR partition hierarchy is preserved across temperatures (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-temp-stability}).


\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

We next ask whether existing detection methods---designed for binary real-vs-synthetic classification---incidentally capture depth structure, repurposing four detection scores as ordinal depth predictors under identical XGBoost and LOGO conditions (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).
The strongest baseline is a 4-detector + surface feature ensemble (binary F1\,=\,0.708), which pools four detection scores with TTR and hapax ratio; among individual detectors, Avg-LogProb provides the most meaningful single-feature comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Depth classification (8-gen LOGO, pooled evaluation; pooled F1 is slightly higher than per-generator macro F1 in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results} because larger test folds from high-performing generators contribute proportionally more). Identical XGBoost configuration. 7-feature: one 410M model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles: two models, 1.4B params (2{,}868\,MB). Individual detection scores (N/A) share the reference-model forward pass; per-method timing is not separately profiled. XGBoost uses 7 handcrafted scalar features (one 410M reference); QLoRA (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}) uses the full token sequence---an information-asymmetric ceiling, not a fair comparison.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Feat.} & \textbf{3-class F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{ms/text} \\
\midrule
4-det.\ + surface     & 6 & \textbf{0.795} & \textbf{0.708} & 22.8 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 4 & 0.785 & 0.700 & 22.8 \\
7-feature (ours)      & 7 & 0.774 & 0.705 & \textbf{9.7} \\
\midrule
Avg-LogProb           & 1 & 0.707 & 0.673 & --- \\
FastDetectGPT         & 1 & 0.660 & 0.569 & --- \\
LD-Score              & 1 & 0.630 & 0.675 & --- \\
Binoculars            & 1 & 0.411 & 0.539 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The 7-feature ensemble matches the 4-detector+surface ensemble on binary F1 (0.705 vs.\ 0.708; Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}) at ${\sim}2.4\times$ lower cost, though the latter leads by 2.1\,pp on 3-class F1 (0.795 vs.\ 0.774); both surpass individual detectors by 3.0--16.6\,pp.
DDR's contribution is the decomposition into location-sensitive and shape-stable components (\S\ref{sec:discussion}), achieving comparable depth discrimination with a single 410M reference model; ordinal-specific methods (CORAL, Frank-Hall) yield comparable F1 (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}).
Concurrent iterative-generation studies use different chain operators (back-translation, rephrasing) and target different phenomena (factuality, convergence dynamics), precluding direct inclusion as depth classification baselines (Appendix~\ref{app:iterative-comparison}).

\vspace{-3mm}
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Mechanistic interpretation.}
\looseness=-1 DDR reflects an empirical regularity of base-model autoregressive generation: recursive inputs compress the token-level NLL distribution monotonically (Appendix~\ref{app:nll-distribution}), shifting location features independently of the token-selection mechanism.
Shape features resist this compression because they are architecturally bounded---entropy max is capped by $\log|V|$, and tail decay reflects softmax long-tail geometry rather than input statistics---explaining their near-stationarity across generators and depths.
Controlled ablations (Appendices~\ref{app:decoding-strategy}--\ref{app:prompt-type}) and ANOVA corroborate this: $\eta^2_{\text{depth}}{=}0.34$--$0.37$ for location vs.\ $\eta^2_{\text{gen}}{<}0.009$ (${>}48{\times}$; Appendix~\ref{app:variance-decomposition}; Granger causality: Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-causality}), indicating that generation depth is a more fundamental analytical dimension than generator architecture---recursive generation imposes a universal compression trajectory that transcends model family.
DDR is distinct from model collapse \cite{curse_of_recursion,strong_model_collapse}---retraining erodes tail via weight updates, while DDR shifts only location---and complements concurrent iterative-generation studies (Appendix~\ref{app:iterative-comparison}).

\paragraph{Failure modes and practical scope.}
\looseness=-1 The gen-1$\to$gen-2 boundary is the primary classification failure point (depth-2 error rate\,=\,48.4\%; Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis}): the gen-0$\to$gen-1 transition produces the largest distributional shift, so depth-2 texts carry insufficient accumulated drift beyond gen-1 for reliable separation.
Deeper generations ($\geq$gen-3) show monotonically decreasing error rates ($\leq$29.2\%), confirming that DDR signal strengthens with chain length.
Instruction tuning compresses DDR (QLoRA: F1\,=\,0.89--0.91; Appendix~\ref{app:instruct}), while cross-domain signal persists (F1\,=\,0.74--0.75; Appendix~\ref{app:domain}).
\vspace{-2mm}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

An estimated 7--17\% of recent scientific publications already contain substantial LLM-modified content \cite{mapping_llm_science,monitoring_ai_review}. As these outputs re-enter training corpora \cite{dolma,fineweb} and seed further generation, text accumulates at varying \emph{generation depths}; each successive cycle reshapes distributional properties in ways that matter for data provenance, corpus auditing, and contamination monitoring in base-model pretraining corpora \cite{ccnet,dsir} as well as model collapse analysis \cite{curse_of_recursion,strong_model_collapse}. Understanding \emph{how} recursive generation alters text distributions---and which properties change versus which are preserved---is therefore a prerequisite for principled corpus curation, yet current methods address only binary human-or-machine detection \cite{detectgpt,fast_detectgpt,binoculars,ld_score}.
Base models provide the cleanest substrate to isolate depth effects from alignment-induced changes.
We find that recursive generation produces a decomposable depth profile: distributional \emph{location} drifts with depth while \emph{shape} remains stable across generators, an asymmetry sufficient for cross-generator depth prediction.

Distributional compression under recursive generation is well-documented \cite{curious_decline,broken_telephone,markovian_chains}, yet existing analyses characterize \emph{that} and \emph{why} convergence occurs without decomposing what drifts from what is preserved.
We introduce \emph{depth profiling} (coarse ordinal classification into gen-0\,/\,gen-1\,/\,gen-2+), and decompose this compression into structurally distinct components: across eight base-model generators spanning five architectural families (0.6B--8B), the NLL distribution's central tendency (mean entropy (reference-model NLL), lexical diversity) drifts monotonically with depth while its shape (entropy extremes, tail structure) remains ${\geq}8{\times}$ more stable (effect size $d{=}0.74$--$1.05$ vs.\ $d < 0.10$ for three near-stationary features; entropy std is intermediate ($d{=}0.46$); boundary features show additional nuance; convergent effect-size measures confirm this partition).
We term this location/shape asymmetry the \emph{differential depth response} (DDR; Figure~\ref{fig:ddr-trajectory}), an empirical regularity of base-model autoregressive generation that is mechanistically distinct from model collapse \cite{curse_of_recursion}, which erodes tail coverage via weight updates; DDR instead analyzes fixed-parameter inference chains.
The stationary shape component complements location drift for depth prediction without per-generator calibration.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_ddr_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Feature trajectories across generation depth ($y$-axis: $z$-scored feature values). \textbf{Top row (drifting)}: entropy mean and hapax ratio decline monotonically across all eight generators ($|\rho| > 0.59$). \textbf{Bottom row (stationary)}: entropy max and tail decay remain flat ($|\rho| < 0.07$). Colored lines: individual generators; diamonds: cross-generator mean (blue for drifting, amber for stationary). This dichotomy defines the DDR.}
\label{fig:ddr-trajectory}
\end{figure*}
We focus on base (pretrained) models; instruction tuning degrades depth signal (Appendix~\ref{app:instruct}).

As a proof of concept, a lightweight 7-feature ensemble informed by DDR achieves leak-free binary F1\,=\,0.694 under leave-one-generator-out evaluation, matching multi-detector ensembles at ${\sim}2.4\times$ lower cost; an end-to-end QLoRA classifier reaches 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 (6-gen LOGO; Appendix~\ref{app:qlora}), confirming that the depth signal extends well beyond handcrafted features.

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Empirical decomposition (DDR).} Across eight generators from five families, we decompose inference-chain drift into two primary response zones---drifting ($d{=}0.74$--$1.05$) vs.\ near-stationary ($d < 0.10$)---plus one intermediate feature ($d{=}0.46$). Where prior work documents \emph{what converges} \cite{curious_decline,markovian_chains}, DDR identifies \emph{what is approximately preserved}, an empirical regularity with analytical utility for depth classification without per-generator calibration. DDR is a descriptive empirical finding; the classification value lies in this decomposition rather than the classifier itself.
    \item \textbf{Task formulation and evaluation suite.} We define generation depth classification as an ordinal prediction task and construct a controlled dataset of 37K texts from eight generators across five families (0.6B--8B; exploratory evidence extends to 14B, Appendix~\ref{app:9gen-scale}), with leave-one-generator-out cross-validation as the primary evaluation protocol.
    \item \textbf{Scope characterization.} Systematic evaluation across temperature, prompt type, instruction tuning, model scale, domain transfer, and perturbation robustness maps the operational envelope of depth profiling.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{-1mm}
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Seven distributional features. ``Entropy'' features are computed as per-token NLL under Pythia-410M (see \S\ref{sec:features}); surface features require no reference model. DDR analysis (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) reveals that entropy mean and the two surface features (TTR, hapax ratio) drift with depth while entropy max, min, and tail decay remain near-stationary; entropy std is intermediate. All ``entropy'' features are reference-model NLL values (cross-entropy with Pythia-410M), not information-theoretic entropy; see \S\ref{sec:features}.}
\label{tab:features}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{3.0cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Source} & \textbf{Measures} \\
\midrule
\texttt{entropy\_mean} & Ref.\ NLL & Central tendency of token surprise \\
\texttt{entropy\_std}  & Ref.\ NLL & Spread of token surprise \\
\texttt{entropy\_max}  & Ref.\ NLL & Maximum token surprise \\
\texttt{entropy\_min}  & Ref.\ NLL & Minimum token surprise \\
\texttt{tail\_decay}   & Ref.\ NLL & Log-prob decay slope (ranks 100--1K) \\
\midrule
\texttt{ttr}           & Surface & Type-token ratio \\
\texttt{hapax\_ratio}  & Surface & Proportion of hapax legomena \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Task Formulation}
\label{sec:task}

Given a text $x$, we predict its \emph{generation depth} $d \in \{0, 1, 2{+}\}$: human-written ($d{=}0$), first-generation LLM output ($d{=}1$), or text produced by two or more recursive LLM generations ($d{=}2{+}$).
A generation chain of depth $k$ is defined recursively: $x^{(0)} \sim \mathcal{C}_0$ (human corpus), $x^{(k)} = G(x^{(k-1)})$ for $k \geq 1$, where $G$ denotes stochastic autoregressive generation.
Depths beyond 2 are merged into $2{+}$ because feature convergence compresses inter-class margins at deeper levels (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}).

\subsection{Generation Chain Construction}
\label{sec:generation}

We construct generation chains using eight generators spanning five model families: \textbf{Pythia} (2.8B, 1B) \cite{pythia}, \textbf{OLMo} (OLMo-2-1B) \cite{olmo}, \textbf{Qwen} (Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-8B-Base) \cite{qwen3}, \textbf{Llama} (Llama-3.2-3B) \cite{llama3}, and \textbf{Mistral} (Mistral-7B-v0.3) \cite{mistral7b}.
These generators span five distinct architectures with a 13$\times$ parameter range (0.6B--8B), trained on heterogeneous corpora; Qwen contributes three models (0.6B--8B) covering the full scale range; Qwen's overrepresentation (3/8 generators) is a limitation, but LOFO holding out all three Qwen models (F1\,=\,0.686) provides the strictest family-level test.

For each generator, we sample approximately 1{,}000 seed passages from English Wikipedia as $\mathcal{C}_0$.
The same pool of seed passages serves as gen-0 for all generators.
We use shared seeds across generators so that cross-generator comparisons control for input content and isolate the effect of the generation model from source text variation.
In LOGO evaluation, gen-0 samples from the held-out generator's chains also appear (with the same text) in the training generators' chains, creating a potential train--test overlap for the gen-0 class.
We address this with a supplementary binary evaluation excluding gen-0 entirely (\S\ref{sec:results-baselines}).
Each seed is iteratively fed through the generator to produce a chain of depth 4 (i.e., gen-0 through gen-4), using the prompt ``\texttt{Continue the following text: \{text\}}''.
We generate up to depth~4 to empirically confirm convergence: 5-class analysis shows adjacent depths 2--4 become indistinguishable (F1\,$\approx$\,0.27; Appendix~\ref{app:finer-depth}), motivating the gen-2+ class collapse.
All generation uses temperature $\tau{=}0.7$, nucleus sampling with $p{=}0.9$, and maximum length of 256 new tokens.
After filtering chain breaks and passages too short for feature extraction, 37{,}058 texts remain across all depths (Appendix~\ref{app:dataset-stats}); depths 2--4 are collapsed into $2{+}$.

\subsection{Feature Extraction}
\label{sec:features}

\paragraph{Terminology.} Throughout this paper, we refer to per-token cross-entropy loss under a fixed reference model as ``entropy'' for conciseness; these are reference-model NLL values, not true information-theoretic entropy of the generating distribution.

We deliberately use interpretable distributional statistics so that the DDR partition remains transparent; end-to-end neural features improve classification ($+$11.6\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:qlora}) but obscure the decomposition.
Leave-one-feature-out analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}) confirms that both reference-model and surface feature groups contribute independently.

We extract seven distributional features per text (Table~\ref{tab:features}), covering the NLL distribution's location, spread, extremes, and tail structure, plus two reference-free surface diversity measures; six additional NLL statistics were evaluated and rejected for redundancy ($|r| > 0.65$, $\Delta$F1\,$\leq$\,$-0.011$; Appendix~\ref{app:feature-candidate}).

\paragraph{Reference-model features.}
A held-out reference model (Pythia-410M, chosen to be smaller than all generators) computes per-token negative log-likelihood (NLL) for each text.
Although Pythia-410M shares the GPT-NeoX architecture with two of the eight generators, cross-family reference validation (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-ref}) shows F1 spread of only 0.013 across Pythia, OLMo, and Qwen references, confirming DDR is reference-model agnostic; however, all tested references are causal LMs.
From the resulting NLL sequence, we extract five statistics: mean, standard deviation, maximum, and minimum of the token-level NLL, plus tail\_decay---the slope of the vocabulary-level averaged log-probability distribution in ranks 100--1000, capturing the power-law regime where depth-induced compression is most detectable while avoiding high-frequency head noise and sparse extreme-tail counts.

\paragraph{Surface features.}
Two features capture lexical diversity without requiring a reference model: type-token ratio (TTR) and the proportion of words appearing exactly once (hapax\_ratio).

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
These features exhibit a \emph{differential depth response} (DDR), separating into drifting and non-drifting groups (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}; pairwise correlations in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-feature-correlation}).
TTR and hapax ratio are strongly correlated ($r = 0.97$); both are retained for marginal LOFO gains.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Cross-validation.}
We evaluate generalization via \textbf{Leave-One-Generator-Out (LOGO)} cross-validation: each fold holds out one generator entirely, so the classifier must generalize to a generator absent from training---the strictest per-model protocol for assessing deployment against previously unseen generators.
LOGO does not isolate family-level effects; \textbf{Leave-One-Family-Out (LOFO)} addresses this by holding out entire model families (binary F1\,=\,0.686; \S\ref{sec:results-classification}).
Generator subsets vary across experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:gen-subsets-section}).

\paragraph{Classifier.}
We use XGBoost \cite{xgboost} throughout ($n\_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max\_\text{depth}{=}6$, learning rate $0.1$; fixed without tuning), with identical hyperparameters across all methods in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}.
Alternative classifiers (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, CORN \cite{corn_ordinal}, Frank-Hall \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}) yield comparable performance (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}); discriminative power resides in the features, not the classifier.

\paragraph{Metrics and statistical testing.}
The primary metric is \textbf{macro-averaged F1} over the three depth classes, with 95\% bootstrap CIs ($n{=}1{,}000$).
All $p$-values are two-sided with Bonferroni correction within each family of simultaneous tests.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare the 7-feature ensemble against four single-score detection methods repurposed as depth predictors: Avg-LogProb, FastDetectGPT \cite{fast_detectgpt}, Binoculars \cite{binoculars}, and LD-Score \cite{ld_score}.
Each baseline score is used as a univariate feature in the same XGBoost framework under LOGO cross-validation to ensure a controlled comparison.

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Since TTR and hapax\_ratio are mechanically length-dependent, we verify depth signal is not a length artifact via length-matched subsampling that equalizes length distributions across depth classes (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}; Appendix~\ref{app:length}).

